The [full paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1702.02885) proves worst-case hardness for Label-Cover-derived dictionaries; it does not support Track A hardness for inverse-planted sparse vectors. The attempted Track B family passed G1–G8 locally, but the official oracle loop found:

- Easy: 3/3 solved
- Medium: 3/3 solved
- Hard: 2/3 solved

The harness returned `too_easy`. Further escalation would exceed G9’s 300-operation cap, so I stopped without tuning or running the hinted/placebo arms.

